expansions = {
    '2m_q32': '<p>The formula for Root Mean Squared Error is <code>RMSE = &radic;((1/n) &Sigma; (yi - &ycirc;i)&sup2;)</code>. RMSE provides a measure of the average magnitude of the error. Its primary advantage over Mean Squared Error (MSE) is that taking the square root converts the error metric back into the same units as the target variable, making it much easier to interpret. Furthermore, like MSE, it heavily penalizes larger errors because the errors are squared before being averaged, which is highly beneficial when large errors are particularly undesirable.</p>',
    '2m_q33': '<p>The R-squared (R&sup2;) coefficient, also known as the coefficient of determination, measures the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that is predictably explained by the independent variables in a regression model. Its standard range lies between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates that the model explains none of the variability and 1 indicates it explains all of it. A higher R-squared value generally indicates a better fit for the model, although it should be evaluated in context with other metrics.</p>',
    '2m_q34': '<p>Standard R-squared artificially inflates whenever a new independent variable is added to a model, even if that variable does not meaningfully improve the predictions. Adjusted R-squared is preferred because it incorporates a penalty for adding non-significant predictors, thereby adjusting for the number of terms in the model. This provides a more accurate and reliable measure of goodness-of-fit for multiple linear regression models, preventing overfitting by ensuring only useful variables increase the score.</p>',
    '2m_q35': '<p>The mathematical formula for the Sigmoid activation function is <code>&sigma;(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))</code>. This function maps any real-valued number into a continuously bounded output range strictly between 0 and 1. Because of this specific S-shaped output range, it is ideal for tasks such as binary classification and probability estimation, as the output can be directly interpreted as the probability of a data point belonging to the positive class.</p>',
    '2m_q36': '<p>In Logistic Regression for binary classification, the standard decision threshold value is typically set to 0.5. If the model\'s predicted probability for an instance is greater than or equal to 0.5, the instance is classified into the positive class (class 1). Conversely, if the predicted probability is strictly less than 0.5, it is classified into the negative class (class 0). This threshold can be adjusted depending on the specific requirements of precision and recall for the problem.</p>',
    '2m_q37': '<p>The single-sample log-loss, also known as binary cross-entropy, is calculated using the formula <code>Cost = - [y * log(&ycirc;) + (1 - y) * log(1 - &ycirc;)]</code>. In this equation, <code>y</code> represents the true binary label of the sample (either 0 or 1), and <code>&ycirc;</code> represents the model\'s predicted probability that the sample belongs to class 1. This cost function heavily penalizes highly confident but incorrect predictions, guiding the model toward more accurate probability estimates.</p>',
    '2m_q38': '<p>A decision boundary in a binary classification problem is a geometric hypersurface that separates the underlying feature space into two distinct regions, one for each class. Depending on the algorithm and data, this boundary can be linear (like a hyperplane in SVM or Logistic Regression) or non-linear (like in Decision Trees or RBF-kernel SVMs). Any new data point falling on one side of this boundary is assigned to the first class, while points on the other side are assigned to the second class.</p>',
    '2m_q39': '<p>The K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) algorithm operates as a non-parametric, instance-based learning method. When tasked with classifying a new, unseen data point, the algorithm calculates the distance between this point and all other points in the training dataset using a metric like Euclidean distance. It then identifies the \'K\' closest data points (neighbors) and assigns the new point to the class that represents the statistical mode (majority vote) among those K neighbors.</p>',
    '2m_q40': '<p>The formula for Euclidean distance between two points, p and q, in an n-dimensional feature space is <code>d(p, q) = &radic;(&Sigma; (q_i - p_i)&sup2;)</code> for i=1 to n. This metric calculates the length of the straight-line path connecting the two points. It is widely used as the default distance metric in various machine learning algorithms, including K-Nearest Neighbors and K-Means clustering, to quantify the similarity or dissimilarity between data instances.</p>',
    '2m_q41': '<p>Setting the parameter K too small in the KNN algorithm causes the model to become overly sensitive to local noise and outliers in the training data, leading to severe overfitting and complex decision boundaries. Conversely, setting K too large causes the decision boundary to become overly smooth and generalized, leading to underfitting as the model fails to capture intricate local patterns. A balanced K value must be chosen, typically via cross-validation, to achieve optimal generalization.</p>',
    '2m_q42': '<p>In Information Theory, Entropy is a fundamental metric used to quantify the level of uncertainty, impurity, or randomness within a given dataset. The mathematical equation for Entropy is <code>H(S) = - &Sigma; p_i * log2(p_i)</code>, where <code>p_i</code> represents the proportion or probability of elements belonging to a specific class i within the set S. A completely pure dataset has an entropy of 0, while a dataset with equally distributed classes has maximum entropy.</p>',
    '2m_q43': '<p>Information Gain measures the expected reduction in Entropy (or impurity) that is achieved by partitioning a dataset according to a specific attribute. In the context of building Decision Trees, Information Gain plays a critical role as the primary criterion for selecting the best feature to split on at each internal node. The algorithm evaluates all available features and selects the one that yields the highest Information Gain, thereby maximizing the homogeneity of the resulting child nodes.</p>',
    '2m_q44': '<p>The Gini Impurity formula, commonly used in CART decision trees, is defined as <code>Gini = 1 - &Sigma; (p_i)&sup2;</code>, where <code>p_i</code> is the probability of an item belonging to class i. This metric quantifies the likelihood of a randomly chosen element from the set being incorrectly labeled if it were randomly labeled according to the distribution of labels in the subset. A Gini Impurity of 0 indicates perfect purity, while higher values indicate a more diverse mix of classes.</p>',
    '2m_q45': '<p>Bayes\' Theorem is mathematically stated as <code>P(A|B) = [P(B|A) * P(A)] / P(B)</code>. In this context, <b>P(A|B)</b> represents the posterior probability of class A given predictor B. <b>P(B|A)</b> is the likelihood, which is the probability of predictor B given class A. <b>P(A)</b> represents the prior probability of class A occurring independently, and <b>P(B)</b> is the marginal probability or evidence of predictor B occurring.</p>',
    '2m_q46': '<p>The Naive Bayes classifier is termed "naive" because it relies on the very strong, and often mathematically unrealistic, assumption of conditional independence among all features. Specifically, it assumes that the presence or value of one particular feature is entirely unrelated to the presence or value of any other feature, given the class label. Despite this simplification, the algorithm frequently performs surprisingly well in real-world complex applications like text classification and spam filtering.</p>',
    '2m_q47': '<p>In the context of Support Vector Machines (SVM), a Hyperplane is the geometric, linear decision boundary that separates the feature space into regions corresponding to different classes. Support Vectors are the critical, specific data points from the training set that lie closest to the hyperplane. These vectors directly dictate the exact position and orientation of the hyperplane; if they were removed or altered, the boundary\'s position would shift significantly.</p>',
    '2m_q48': '<p>Hard Margin SVM strictly enforces that a hyperplane must perfectly separate classes without allowing any misclassifications, making it highly susceptible to overfitting and completely failing if the data is not perfectly linearly separable. In contrast, Soft Margin SVM introduces a hyperparameter (usually denoted as C) and slack variables to intentionally allow a certain degree of misclassification or margin violations. This approach greatly improves the model\'s robustness and generalization when dealing with noisy, overlapping real-world data.</p>',
    '2m_q49': '<p>Kernel functions, such as the Radial Basis Function (RBF) or Polynomial kernels, are mathematically utilized in SVM to compute the dot product of data points as if they were projected into a transformed, higher-dimensional feature space. The "Kernel Trick" allows the algorithm to perform this calculation without explicitly computing the coordinates of the data in that higher dimension. This enables SVMs to efficiently discover highly complex, non-linear decision boundaries that perfectly separate non-linearly separable input data.</p>',
    '2m_q50': '<p>Random Forests are ensemble models that construct a multitude of individual decision trees during the training phase, with each tree built independently using a bootstrapped subset of the training data and a random subset of features at each split. During the prediction phase for classification tasks, each individual tree outputs a predicted class label for a given input. The final classification output of the entire Random Forest is then determined by aggregating these predictions and taking a democratic majority vote among all the trees.</p>',
    '2m_q51': '<p>Three widely used and powerful boosting ensemble algorithms in statistical classification include AdaBoost (Adaptive Boosting), Gradient Boosting Machine (GBM), and XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting). AdaBoost sequentially adjusts the weights of misclassified instances to improve subsequent weak learners. GBM builds trees sequentially by minimizing a differentiable loss function via gradient descent. XGBoost is a highly optimized, scalable, and regularized implementation of gradient boosting that often yields state-of-the-art performance.</p>',
    '2m_q52': '<p>The mathematical formula for Precision is <code>Precision = TP / (TP + FP)</code>, which measures the accuracy of positive predictions. Recall is calculated as <code>Recall = TP / (TP + FN)</code>, quantifying the model\'s ability to find all actual positive instances. The F1-Score is the harmonic mean of both metrics, defined as <code>F1-Score = 2 * (Precision * Recall) / (Precision + Recall)</code>, providing a balanced measure of a model\'s performance, especially crucial for imbalanced datasets.</p>',
    '3m_q1': '<ul><li><b>Supervised Learning:</b> This paradigm relies on fully labeled training datasets, where each input instance is paired with its corresponding desired output or target variable. The objective is to train a model to accurately map inputs to outputs (e.g., classification, regression) so it can generalize to unseen data.</li><li><b>Unsupervised Learning:</b> In contrast, this approach utilizes completely unlabeled datasets, meaning the system is not provided with predefined "correct" answers. The model\'s goal is to autonomously discover hidden structures, inherent patterns, or natural groupings within the data (e.g., clustering, dimensionality reduction).</li><li><b>Reinforcement Learning:</b> This framework involves an autonomous agent that learns to make a sequence of decisions by actively interacting with a dynamic environment. The agent performs actions and receives feedback in the form of rewards or penalties, adjusting its policy over time to maximize its cumulative long-term reward.</li></ul>',
    '3m_q2': '<ul><li><b>1. Data Collection:</b> The foundational stage involves strategically sourcing, aggregating, and extracting raw data from diverse sources such as databases, APIs, web scraping, or sensor logs to build a comprehensive dataset.</li><li><b>2. Data Preparation and Cleaning:</b> This critical phase focuses on enhancing data quality by meticulously handling missing values (imputation or deletion), identifying and removing duplicate records, correcting formatting inconsistencies, and systematically addressing statistical outliers.</li><li><b>3. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA):</b> In this stage, data scientists leverage statistical summaries and visual tools (like scatter plots, histograms, and correlation matrices) to deeply investigate data distributions, uncover underlying patterns, and identify relationships between variables before formal modeling begins.</li></ul>',
    '3m_q3': '<ul><li><b>Importance of Data Cleaning:</b> High-quality data is the bedrock of robust machine learning; the principle of "garbage in, garbage out" dictates that feeding noisy, inconsistent, or corrupted data into an algorithm will inevitably result in poor model accuracy and unreliable predictions. Cleaning ensures the integrity of the modeling process.</li><li><b>Strategy 1: Statistical Imputation:</b> This involves systematically replacing missing values with calculated estimates, such as the mean, median, or mode of the respective column. It is highly effective for preserving the overall dataset size while maintaining the general statistical distribution of the feature.</li><li><b>Strategy 2: Deletion (Listwise or Pairwise):</b> When a row or column contains an overwhelmingly high percentage of missing data, it may be entirely removed from the dataset. While this prevents the introduction of artificial bias from imputation, it must be used cautiously to avoid significant loss of valuable information.</li></ul>',
    '3m_q4': '<p>Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) is a highly iterative and critical phase in the data science pipeline where analysts use a combination of descriptive statistics and visual techniques to systematically summarize the fundamental characteristics of a dataset. The primary goal is to intuitively understand the data\'s structure, uncover underlying patterns, and formulate hypotheses before applying complex predictive models. Data visualization techniques are paramount in EDA; for example, histograms effectively illustrate the frequency distribution and skewness of continuous variables, while box plots provide a clear visual summary of a variable\'s central tendency, spread, and statistical outliers. Scatter plots are instrumental in identifying linear or non-linear correlations between pairs of continuous features. By translating raw numerical data into visual formats, EDA enables practitioners to detect anomalies, verify assumptions, and make informed decisions about subsequent feature engineering and preprocessing steps.</p>',
    '3m_q5': '<ul><li><b>Feature Engineering:</b> This is the creative and often domain-specific process of constructing entirely new features or mathematically transforming existing ones to better highlight underlying patterns for the learning algorithm. Examples include creating polynomial features, combining date and time fields into "day of the week," or applying logarithmic transformations to handle skewed data. Its goal is to enhance the model\'s predictive power by providing more informative inputs.</li><li><b>Feature Selection:</b> Conversely, feature selection is a systematic reduction process aimed at identifying and retaining only the most relevant, original features while explicitly discarding redundant, noisy, or irrelevant ones. Techniques include correlation analysis, recursive feature elimination, or utilizing feature importances from tree-based models. Its primary goals are to simplify the model, significantly reduce training time, combat the curse of dimensionality, and mitigate the risk of overfitting.</li></ul>',
    '3m_q6': '<p>Categorical label encoding is an absolutely fundamental preprocessing step because the vast majority of mathematical algorithms and optimization techniques used in machine learning (such as gradient descent or distance metrics) strictly require continuous numerical input matrices to perform their calculations. Raw categorical data, which typically consists of text strings or discrete labels (e.g., "Red", "Green", "Blue" or "Low", "Medium", "High"), cannot be directly processed by these algebraic equations. Therefore, techniques like Label Encoding (which assigns a unique integer to each category, useful for ordinal data) or One-Hot Encoding (which creates binary dummy variables for each category, preventing the model from assuming false ordinality in nominal data) must be applied to translate these qualitative concepts into quantitative representations. Failing to encode categorical variables will result in immediate execution errors or severe misinterpretations by the model.</p>',
    '3m_q7': '<ul><li><b>Feature Scaling:</b> This technique involves linearly transforming numeric features so that they fall within a specific, strictly bounded standard range, most commonly between 0 and 1 (Min-Max scaling). This is particularly useful for algorithms that do not make assumptions about the distribution of the data, such as neural networks or distance-based models like KNN.</li><li><b>Feature Normalization (Standardization):</b> Often referred to as Z-score normalization, this process transforms the data distribution to center around a mean of zero with a standard deviation of one. It effectively handles outliers better than min-max scaling and is highly recommended for optimization algorithms like Gradient Descent (used in SVMs and Logistic Regression) to ensure smooth convergence.</li><li><b>Purpose:</b> Both techniques are critically applied before model fitting to ensure that features with naturally large magnitudes (e.g., salary in thousands) do not disproportionately dominate the objective function or distance calculations compared to features with smaller magnitudes (e.g., age in decades), ensuring fair and balanced learning across all variables.</li></ul>',
    '3m_q8': '<p>Model training represents the central, computationally intensive learning phase within the machine learning lifecycle where the theoretical algorithm is actively exposed to the prepared training dataset. During this phase, the algorithm iteratively adjusts its internal parameters (such as the weights and biases in a neural network or the coefficients in a linear regression) to systematically minimize a predefined error or loss function. By continuously comparing its predictions against the actual ground-truth labels in supervised learning, the model essentially "learns" the complex mathematical mapping or underlying patterns that define the relationship between the independent input features and the dependent target variable. The success and significance of this phase directly determine the model\'s capacity to generalize its learned rules to accurately predict outcomes on entirely new, unseen data points.</p>',
    '3m_q9': '<ul><li><b>Train-Test Split:</b> This is the most fundamental validation strategy, where the complete dataset is randomly partitioned into two mutually exclusive subsets: a training set (typically 70-80% of the data) used strictly for model fitting, and a distinct testing set (20-30%) held out to simulate real-world, unseen data. The model\'s performance on the testing set provides a baseline estimate of its generalization error and helps detect obvious overfitting.</li><li><b>K-Fold Cross Validation:</b> This is a far more robust and rigorous technique used to maximize data efficiency and ensure stability. The dataset is divided into K equally sized, mutually exclusive subsets or "folds". The training and evaluation process is then repeated K separate times. In each iteration, the model is trained on K-1 folds and validated on the single remaining fold. The final performance metric is calculated as the average of the results across all K iterations, providing a much more reliable and unbiased estimate of model performance, especially on smaller datasets.</li></ul>',
    '3m_q10': '<p>In predictive modeling, understanding the distinction between independent predictor variables and dependent target variables is crucial for defining the problem statement. <b>Independent Predictor Variables</b> (also known as features, inputs, or X) are the measurable attributes or characteristics of the data that the model uses to make its prediction. These are the variables that you already know or can easily observe. Conversely, the <b>Dependent Target Variable</b> (also known as the label, output, or y) is the specific outcome or value that the model is actively attempting to forecast or classify based on those predictors. For example, in a medical diagnosis scenario aimed at predicting heart disease, the independent predictor variables would include patient metrics such as age, resting blood pressure, cholesterol levels, and family history. The dependent target variable would be the final diagnosis—a binary classification of whether the patient actually has heart disease (Yes/No). The model learns how changes in the independent variables mathematically influence the dependent variable.</p>'
}

